Inheritance:
	Inheritance is an important pillar of OOP. It is the mechanism in java by which one class is allowed to inherit the features.
	In Java, Inheritance means creating a new classes based on existing ones. A class that inherits from another class can reuse the methods and fields of that classes. You can add new fields methods to current class as well as.
	
Syntax: 
class ChildClass extends ParentClass{

}
Example : 
class Student extends Person{

}

Types of Inheritance:
	1.Single Inheritance
	2.Multiple Inheritance
	3.Multilevel Inheritance
	4.Hierarchical Inheritance
	5.Hybrid Inheritance
	
Single Inheritance:
	Single Inheritance, SubClass inherit  the features of one super class, Class A serves as a base for the derived class B.
	Person
	   |
	Student
	(Person Properties)
	->name
	->age
	(student Properties)
	->rollNumber

Multiple Inheritance :
	Multiple Inheritance, one class can have more than one superClass and inherits features from all parent classes.
	Example :
		Father   	Mother
		   |              |
		       |      |
		         Child

Multilevel Inheritance:
	Multilevel Inheritance, A derived Class will be inheriting a base class and as well as the derived class also the base class for the other classes
		Example : 
		GrandFather
		     |
		   Father
		     |
		    Son

Hierarchical Inheritance :
	In Hierarchical Inheritance , One class serves as a superclass(Base Class) for more than one subclass.
	Example:
		Person
	     |	         |
	 Student      Teacher
	 
	Person Properties : name, age
	Student : rollNumber
	Teacher : salary

Hybrid Inheritance : 
	It is a mixed of two or more of the above types of inheritance.
	Java doesn't support multiple inheritance with classes, hybrid inheritance involving multiple inheritance
also not possible with classes.
	We can achieve hybrid inheritance only through interface.
	
